resources.string:
Willbolt's accessibility service can see what is on your screen. It reads the name of the app that is open, the text on the screen (including a browser's address bar) and, if you turn the screen check on, a screenshot every few seconds. It uses that to:

• block the apps and websites you chose, and count your time in apps for your limits and breaks;
• check the screenshots for adult content, with a model that runs on this phone;
• press Home or lock the screen when something is blocked.

None of it is stored or sent anywhere: screenshots are looked at in memory and thrown away, and there are no accounts, ads or analytics. You can switch the service off at any time in Android's Accessibility settings.
